const faq = [
  ['What are the best foods for glowing skin?', 'I focus on protein at every meal, healthy fats such as olive oil, avocado and eggs, fatty fish, and plenty of plants: berries, vegetables, oats, seeds and legumes. Consistency matters more than any single food.'],
  ['Can food really change my skin?', 'Food gives your skin and gut the raw materials for repair, and what you eat can influence inflammation. It will not replace sunscreen, gentle skincare or medical treatment, and results vary a lot from person to person.'],
  ['Which supplements are good for skin?', 'Magnesium, omega-3s, zinc and probiotics are the ones people ask about most. They can assist, but evidence is mixed, doses matter, and I would ask a doctor or pharmacist first and keep real food as the base.'],
  ['How much zinc is too much?', 'The US National Institutes of Health sets the adult upper limit at 40 mg of zinc per day from all sources. Too much can cause nausea and, over time, low copper and lower HDL cholesterol, so zinc is a short-term, doctor-guided supplement for me, never a mega-dose.'],
  ['Are probiotics good for skin?', 'Research is early but interesting. A review of nine randomized trials in acne found lesions fell after 12 weeks, not at 4 weeks, with no major side effects reported, but the trials were small. I would choose a low dose of gentle strains, introduce them slowly and only if my body tolerates them.'],
  ['Is bone broth good for my gut and skin?', 'Bone broth provides protein and amino acids such as glycine and proline, and many people find it soothing and easy to digest. Direct proof that it heals the gut or improves skin is limited, so I treat it as a nourishing food, not a treatment.'],
  ['How long does it take to see a difference?', 'Skin renews over weeks, not days. I would give any change in food a few weeks, and change one thing at a time, before judging it.'],
];
