<div class="art-head"><div><div class="up" style="color:#8A8A8A">Notes · Glow foods · Gut and skin, part 2 · Updated October 2026</div><h1 class="hd">Foods for Glowing Skin: My Simple Food and Supplements Guide</h1><p class="serif stand">If your skin feels reactive, dry, inflamed or just “off”, sometimes the most helpful thing is to simplify. This is part 2 of my skin barrier and gut health series: the daily food basics I come back to, the foods that feel extra supportive, how I think about supplements, and what I would be careful with.</p><div class="hand" style="transform:rotate(-2deg);font-size:34px;margin-top:10px">big sister advice: keep it simple</div></div>
<div class="ph art-hero"><span>Image 1 · Hero<br><em>Cover collage: pink blender with spinach, kiwi and papaya bowls, cucumber drink selfie, halved papaya</em></span></div></div></section>
<section class="p art-grid"><div class="art-body">

<p>In <a href="/notes/gut-health-and-skin-barrier/">part 1</a> I explained the gut-skin axis. The obvious next question is: <strong>so what should I actually eat?</strong></p>
<p>My answer is less dramatic than most “foods for glowing skin” lists. I do not believe in one superfood. I believe in a steady base of real food that gives your skin barrier and your gut lining the materials they are made of, and in being careful and boring with supplements. This is general information from my own reading and routine, not medical advice.</p>

<h2>Key takeaways</h2>
<ul class="chk">
<li>Your skin barrier is built from proteins and lipids, so protein and healthy fats matter as much as skincare.</li>
<li>Fibre from real plants feeds the gut bacteria that help protect the gut lining.</li>
<li>Eggs, fatty fish, bone broth and small amounts of fermented foods feel extra supportive to me.</li>
<li>Supplements can assist but do not replace food, and zinc and vitamin A have real upper limits.</li>
<li>More is not better. Consistent is better.</li>
</ul>

<h2>The daily basics I start with</h2>
<p>Before I think about any supplement, I look at the basics. Your barrier is built from nutrients, not just skincare.</p>
<ul class="chk">
<li><strong>Protein at every meal.</strong> The skin barrier is made of proteins and lipids, and the gut lining renews itself every few days. Both need amino acids daily to repair.</li>
<li><strong>Healthy fats</strong> such as olive oil, avocado and eggs. They support cell membranes and help reduce water loss from the skin.</li>
<li><strong>Fibre from real plants:</strong> oats, berries, vegetables, legumes, chia. Fibre feeds good bacteria, which make compounds that help protect the gut lining.</li>
<li><strong>Warm, simple meals, especially when stressed.</strong> Easier digestion means less burden on the body.</li>
</ul>
<div class="ph fig3"><span>Image 2<br><em>Daily basics flat lay: oats with berries and chia, avocado, olive oil, eggs</em></span></div>

<h2>Foods that feel extra supportive</h2>
<p>These are the foods I reach for most when my skin feels reactive. The science is a mix of solid nutrition basics and early research, so I treat them as support, not treatment.</p>
<table class="tbl"><tr><th>Food</th><th>Why I like it</th></tr>
<tr><td><em class="nm">Eggs</em></td><td>They contain cholesterol, and yes, your skin barrier needs it: cholesterol is one of the three main lipids in the outer skin layer, along with ceramides and fatty acids. They are also a source of protein and fat-soluble vitamins.</td></tr>
<tr><td><em class="nm">Fatty fish</em></td><td>A source of omega-3s, which help regulate inflammatory pathways. Reviews of omega-3s in skin conditions are mixed, but they support the barrier and are good for overall health.</td></tr>
<tr><td><em class="nm">Bone broth</em></td><td>Provides protein and amino acids such as glycine and proline, building blocks for connective tissue. It is gentle on digestion, though proof that it repairs the gut is limited.</td></tr>
<tr><td><em class="nm">Fermented foods</em></td><td>Yogurt, kefir and sauerkraut in small amounts support microbiome diversity, if you tolerate them.</td></tr></table>
<blockquote class="quote">More is not better, consistent is better.</blockquote>
<div class="figrow"><div class="ph fig45"><span>Image 3<br><em>Eggs and salmon plate</em></span></div><div class="ph fig45"><span>Image 4<br><em>Kefir or yogurt bowl, or a cup of bone broth</em></span></div></div>

<h3>An easy glow smoothie</h3>
<p>A simple way to eat more plants is a smoothie with spinach, kiwi and apple or pear, plus cucumber water with lemon during the day. Spinach brings fibre and folate, kiwi brings vitamin C, and nothing about it is magic. It is just an easy habit. My <a href="/notes/best-foods-for-skin/">grocery list for glowing skin</a> has more ideas.</p>
<div class="ph fig3"><span>Image 5<br><em>Smoothie ingredients in bowls next to the blender, cucumber drink</em></span></div>

<h2>Supplements: my careful approach</h2>
<p><strong>Get medical advice before adding any supplement.</strong> Supplements can interact with medication, and what looks harmless can cause problems at high doses. Here is how I think about the four I get asked about most, with the official numbers from the US National Institutes of Health:</p>
<table class="tbl"><tr><th>Supplement</th><th>How I think about it</th></tr>
<tr><td><em class="nm">Magnesium</em></td><td>I would take it in the evening if at all. It supports the nervous system, sleep and digestion, and stress directly affects the skin barrier. Adult women need about 310–320 mg a day from all sources, and the upper limit for supplements is 350 mg. Too much commonly causes diarrhoea and cramps.</td></tr>
<tr><td><em class="nm">Omega-3s</em></td><td>Help regulate inflammatory signalling and support barrier lipids. I prefer fish, walnuts, chia and flaxseed first. If you take blood thinners, ask your doctor before a fish oil supplement.</td></tr>
<tr><td><em class="nm">Zinc</em></td><td>Helpful for skin repair and immune balance, but short term only. The adult upper limit is 40 mg a day from food and supplements together. Too much can cause nausea and, over time, low copper.</td></tr>
<tr><td><em class="nm">Probiotics</em></td><td>Only if your body tolerates them: low dose, gentle strains, slow introduction. Trials in acne are small and show benefit only after about 12 weeks.</td></tr></table>
<div class="callout"><div class="up">Important</div><p>Supplements assist. They do not replace real food. Please check with a doctor or pharmacist before starting any of these, especially if you are pregnant, breastfeeding, have a medical condition or take medication. Preformed vitamin A in particular should be avoided in high doses during pregnancy.</p></div>

<h2>What to be careful with</h2>
<p>Barrier damage is often caused by overcorrection. These are the things I would watch out for:</p>
<ul class="chk">
<li>high-dose supplements without testing</li>
<li>constantly switching protocols</li>
<li>extreme elimination diets</li>
<li>over-exfoliating while “healing the gut”</li>
<li>treating symptoms without regulating stress</li>
</ul>
<p>The stress link is the part that surprised me most. A review of 21 studies describes how psychological stress activates stress hormones that can lower the skin’s barrier lipids and hydration and increase water loss. So I treat stress care as part of the plan, not an extra.</p>
<blockquote class="quote">Your body heals in safety, not urgency.</blockquote>
<div class="ph fig3"><span>Image 6<br><em>Calm evening: tea, candle, soft light, a quiet moment</em></span></div>

<h2>Think about healing</h2>
<p>The skin barrier and the gut are both sensitive to the nervous system. Here is what I keep in mind:</p>
<ul class="chk">
<li>Calm digestion means better nutrient absorption.</li>
<li>Stable blood sugar supports stable hormones.</li>
<li>Enough protein and fats support a stronger lipid barrier.</li>
<li>Fewer actives mean less barrier disruption. My <a href="/skin-guide/barrier-repair/">barrier repair playbook</a> shows how I keep skincare gentle.</li>
<li>Deep sleep is when cellular repair peaks.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote class="quote">Healing happens when the body feels supported.</blockquote>

<div class="callout"><div class="up">When I would see a doctor</div><p>If you have persistent eczema, severe or painful acne, ongoing digestive symptoms, or you are thinking about supplements or an elimination diet, please talk to a doctor, dermatologist or registered dietitian first. I am not a medical professional.</p></div>

